  "about": {
    "title": "About",
    "columns": [
      "<p><strong>SAIL (Software &amp; AI Engineering Lab)</strong> is a research lab at <strong>PUC-Rio</strong> dedicated to advancing <em>Software and AI Engineering</em> through rigorous, impactful, and industry-connected research. We investigate how to build, evaluate, and improve software and AI-intensive systems, with interests spanning <em>AI Engineering</em>, <em>Empirical Software Engineering</em>, and <em>human aspects of software development</em>. By connecting scientific rigor with real-world needs, SAIL seeks to generate knowledge, methods, and practical solutions that help shape the future of software and AI systems in academia and industry.</p>",
      "<p> <strong>SAIL</strong> brings together researchers and students interested in advancing the engineering of software and AI-intensive systems. We study how these systems are designed, developed, evaluated, and improved, with a strong focus on quality, evidence-based research, and real-world relevance. Our goal is to build knowledge and solutions that not only move research forward, but also help address practical challenges faced by industry and society. In doing so, SAIL serves as a space for collaboration, innovation, and the training of the next generation of software and AI engineering researchers.</p>"
    ]
  },
  "specializationsTitle": "Research Areas",
  "specializations": [
    {
      "title": "AI Engineering",
      "description": "<p>Focuses on the methods, processes, and practices needed to design, develop, evaluate, deploy, and maintain AI-based systems in a reliable and responsible way. It addresses the full lifecycle of AI-intensive systems, from data and models to monitoring, quality, and continuous improvement in real-world settings.</p>",
      "images": [
        {
          "src": "./images/AI Engineering.png",
          "alt": "AI Engineering"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "AI for Software Engineering",
      "description": "<p>Explores how AI techniques can support software engineering activities, such as requirements analysis, coding, testing, maintenance, and developer productivity. This area investigates how intelligent tools and models can enhance development processes and help teams build software more effectively and efficiently.</p>",
      "images": [
        {
          "src": "./images/AI4SE.webp",
          "alt": "AI for Software Engineering"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Empirical Software Engineering",
      "description": "<p>Investigates software engineering through observation, experimentation, and evidence, helping researchers and practitioners better understand how software is built in real-world settings. Its goal is to generate insights grounded in data and practice, supporting more informed methods, decisions, and improvements.</p>",
      "images": [
        {
          "src": "./images/EMSE.png",
          "alt": "Empirical Software Engineering"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Human Aspects of Software Engineering",
      "description": "<p>Examines the people side of software engineering, including collaboration, communication, decision-making, teamwork, and the ways humans interact with processes and technologies. It highlights the social and organizational dimensions that influence how software and AI systems are conceived, developed, and used.</p>",
      "images": [
        {
          "src": "./images/Human Aspects.avif",
          "alt": "Human Aspects of Software Engineering"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "coordinationTitle": "Coordination",
  "coordination": {
    "title": "Coordination",
    "coordinator": {
      "photo": "./images/people/Kalinowski.jpg",
      "bio": ""
    },
    "description": "<p style='margin-top: 0px;'><strong>Marcos Kalinowski</strong> is the coordinator of SAIL PUC-Rio and a faculty member at the Department of Informatics at PUC-Rio. With over 15 years of experience in software engineering research, he leads initiatives in empirical software engineering, AI engineering, and human aspects of software development.</p><p><strong>SAIL Coordination</strong> ensures that our research, partnerships, and collaborative initiatives operate smoothly while maintaining the highest standards of academic rigor and real-world impact. Under his leadership, the coordination team works to connect our research community with industry partners, foster collaboration among researchers and students, and translate our findings into practical solutions that benefit both academia and industry.</p>"
  },
